The session plan is written, but it isn't committed. Git refused the commit because session_plan/ is in .gitignore. I didn't force it with -f, since the harness command ends in || true and seems to expect this. The three files are on disk in session_plan/.

Task 01 — Fixes #1080 (product): /bg kill and /quit will send SIGTERM to the job's process group, wait about 2 seconds, then send SIGKILL. With several jobs running, /quit signals them all first and shares one wait, so it never takes more than about 2 seconds in total. The model's bash tool keeps killing instantly on timeout. The fix extends the existing GroupKill helper in src/tools.rs instead of adding a second one. Planned tests:
• a job that catches SIGTERM gets time to clean up;
• a job that ignores SIGTERM still dies, along with its child processes;
• a job that already finished gets no signal;
• /quit with several stubborn jobs stays fast.

Before planning I checked that no script or workflow uses /bg, and that nothing in src, docs or skills points at #1080.

Task 02 — Part of #1012 (product, backlog drain): this is the oldest open issue I filed for myself. None of the remaining wrong prices are for models in yoyo's model picker; all 48 only matter when someone types --model by hand. I decided the slot is still worth it, because /cost would otherwise show a made-up price for a model the user named. This slice gives exact prices to the gemini 3.x flash models that currently get a 0.15/0.60 estimate, checked against Google's own pricing page. Then it re-runs the price audit, where the drift count must drop by exactly the number fixed. It ends with a comment on #1012 giving the commit, the before and after counts, and how many typed-only rows are left. src/format/cost.rs has 42 lines of headroom under the size limit (3563 of 3605), so the new tests go in the separate tests file.

`issue_responses.md`: no community issues today. #1078 and #1071 stay on hold until @yuanhao answers. I flagged one ready slice for the next planner: a test that this repo's CLAUDE.md stays under about 40 KB. It is 41,713 bytes today, so that slice also needs a trim.
